note	Adds the inter-gather row, which prices LVX having no runtime-indexed byte gather at 14.2x. lvx-inter.c built with -DISB_GATHER differs from the inter row by ONE line -- each lane score fetched by _mm_shuffle_epi8 instead of loaded from a precomputed database profile -- and runs 40.588 cycles per cell against 2.850, on identical data, scores, oracle and cell count, with the identical checksum 374781626 asserted before the reference is written. That gap is NOT a toolchain defect and no compiler change can move it: the ISA has only structured UZP/ZIP permutes and no byte gather, so there is nothing for a SIMDe branch to reach, and closing it is an ISA change in lvx-mds. The series is the instrument -- the two rows converge the day such an instruction lands. The two gaps that WERE toolchain defects are both closed: _mm_movemask_epi8 earlier, signed saturating bytes in lvx-simde 369752db (adds_epi8, subs_epi8, subs_epi16 each 11 -> 2 instructions). The striped and inter rows were re-measured against that fix and are byte-identical to before it, 11.386 and 2.850, so the unsigned paths adjacent to the change did not regress. ONE PRECISION NOTE ON provenance=exact: every input tree was clean and all rows are from today, but the snapshot is not a single instant. The polybench TSVs were measured at 09:03:51, lvx-simde moved at 10:29:09, and the ssw TSVs at 10:48:24, so the lvx-simde SHA recorded here postdates the polybench rows. It does not describe them and does not need to: PolyBench is plain C floating-point loop nests and reaches SIMDe nowhere. The compiler, binutils, newlib, ISS and machine-description SHAs are the ones that bear on both suites, and those did not move between the two sweeps.
